# Diagonalization

**Diagonalization** is a proof method: given a list of objects, each of which
can be applied to an index, build a new object by going down the diagonal,
taking what the $n$-th object does at index $n$, and changing it. In this
vault it is the answer to a direct challenge, "isn't a strange loop just
diagonalization?". The answer is no. The two overlap in exactly one place,
Gödel's construction, and each extends beyond the other.

## Description

### The challenge

Once the discussion had settled that the only fully precise strange loop is
Gödel's, the obvious next move was to collapse the notions: if the precise
case is a diagonal argument, perhaps "strange loop" is a loose name for
diagonalization. The reply was *overlap, not equivalence*, argued by going
through the examples one at a time.

| Example | Diagonal? | Strange loop? | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cantor's antidiagonal | yes | no | It produces an object outside a list. Nothing refers to itself and no level is crossed. |
| Gödel's sentence | yes | yes | A sentence refers, through arithmetic coding, to its own provability. |
| Escher, *Drawing Hands* | no | yes | Visual level crossing between the drawn and the drawing. No lemma is applied. |
| Bach's rising canon | no | yes | A musical ascent that returns to its start. No lemma is applied. |

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 subgraph D[Diagonalization]
  C[Cantor-style antidiagonal]
  G[Gödel self-referential fixed point]
 end
 subgraph H[Hofstadter-style strange loops]
  G
  E[Escher's Drawing Hands]
  B[Bach's musical loop]
 end
```

### Two distinctions that keep them apart

- **Method versus interpretive structure.** Diagonalization is something a
  proof *does*. A [[strange-loop]] is something a reader *finds* in a system:
  a hierarchy of levels, a crossing, a return. One is a technique with a
  definite form, the other a pattern recognised across a formal proof, a
  lithograph and a canon.
- **The existence of self-reference versus a theorem about its consequences.**
  That a self-referring sentence exists is one fact, given by the
  [[diagonal-lemma]]. That its existence forces incompleteness is another,
  and needs hypotheses about the theory. A strange loop in Hofstadter's sense
  asks only for the first. The Gödelian refinement asks for the second.

The working formulation that came out of this exchange:

> A strange loop is a self-referential structure arising through
> representational level crossing; Gödelian diagonalization provides a
> canonical formal realization.

### The two forms of the method

The same idea is used in two ways, and only the second produces
self-reference.

- **Antidiagonal: escape a list.** Cantor (1891) takes any list of infinite
  binary sequences and flips the $n$-th digit of the $n$-th sequence. The
  result differs from every entry, so no list is complete. Turing's proof that
  the halting problem is unsolvable and Russell's paradox have the same shape:
  suppose the table is complete, negate the diagonal, derive a contradiction.
  The new object stands *outside* the system.
- **Diagonal fixed point: land inside the list.** Gödel applies a formula to
  its own code, so the result is still a sentence of the theory, one that
  speaks about itself. Here diagonalization is combined with
  [[godel-numbering]], and the outcome is a [[fixed-point]] and not an
  escape.

The first form needs a list and a way to differ. The second also needs the
system to contain names for its own members. That extra requirement, a
representation of the system inside the system, is the part diagonalization
shares with strange loops. Without it a diagonal argument is a counting
argument.

In the taxonomy of self-reference this is class C, formal diagonalization,
whose listed members are the Gödel sentence and Kleene's fixed points. Cantor's
argument is diagonal without belonging to that class.

## Relations

- It is not the same notion as [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a method on one side, an interpretive structure on the other.
- It is the engine of the [[diagonal-lemma]] {relation: generalizes}, its self-referential form.
- It produces the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: uses} when applied to unprovability.
- It needs [[godel-numbering]] {relation: uses} before it can yield self-reference.
- Its self-referential form delivers a [[fixed-point]] {relation: uses} where the antidiagonal form delivers an escape.
- Its oldest instance is the [[cantor-diagonal-argument]] {relation: example-of}, which is diagonal and not a strange loop.
- It is one way of producing [[self-reference]] {relation: uses}, and not the only one.
- It is class C in the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is the mechanism behind [[undecidability]] {relation: uses} of the halting problem.
- It bears on the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}: it marks what the formal case has that the artistic cases lack.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[strange-loop]].** A return to the starting level after moving through a
  hierarchy. Diagonalization supplies its one exact realisation and says
  nothing about the others.
- **[[diagonal-lemma]].** For every property of codes there is a sentence
  provably equivalent to its own code having that property. It is
  diagonalization turned inward.
- **[[fixed-point]].** An object that a transformation sends to itself. The
  inward form of the diagonal argument is the standard way of building one
  where no iteration would converge.
- **[[godel-numbering]].** The coding of formulas and proofs as numbers. It is
  what gives the diagonal something to refer back to.

